Build the scoring table.
- Take the drivers from the previous stage as the rows.
- Assign a weight to each, summing to exactly 100. Weight by what the job actually requires, not by how easy the attribute is to evidence.
- Give me the total and confirm it sums to 100.
- State which two or three attributes are the Floor attributes.
Then apply these two mechanics and restate them back to me so I can check them:

THE SCALE — every score on every attribute uses this and nothing else:
  0     nothing on the page
  1-3   claimed but not evidenced
  4-6   evidenced, but adjacent, junior, or dated
  7-8   evidenced, current, at the level the job needs
  9-10 evidenced, and above the level — would raise the bar of the team they join

THE FLOOR RULE — report two numbers per candidate, never one:
  Weighted score = the sum of score x weight, divided by 100. This ranks.
  Floor          = the lowest score among the Floor attributes. This governs.
  A Floor of 3 or less caps the verdict at reject however high the weighted score.
  There is no averaging out of a hole.

EVIDENCE CAP — anything asserted on a CV without a credit, a link, a named piece of work or a named client is capped at 3, whatever the claim says.

Give me the table for ratification. When I ratify it I will say SETTLED. From that point the weights are the working baseline and are closed to argument for the rest of the batch. If a candidate later arrives who would look better under a different arrangement, that is not a reason to change them — say so and score them as they are.

Closed to argument is not closed to evidence. If you find a concrete contradiction — an attribute the job description does not actually require, a driver that duplicates another, a weight that cannot be evidenced from any CV at all — surface it as DISCONFIRMATION with the evidence, and I will decide.
